Here’s the flow when someone clicks “Mark as Paid” on a commitment circle:

UI handler (DashboardMain)
Method: 
DashboardMain.mark_as_paid(commitment_id, category_name)
 in 
ui/dashboard_main.py
.
Steps:
Calls core.commitment_manager.mark_commitment_paid_manually(user_id, commitment_id) to update the DB.
On success, reloads the commitments grid (
self.load_commitments()
).
Forces a dashboard refresh: 
refresh_dashboard()
, 
refresh_metrics_cards_main()
, and 
metrics_carousel.refresh_metrics_cards()
; these rebuild the balance cards so “Price After Commitments” drops immediately.
Invokes core.commitment_manager.check_commitments(user_id) to refresh due-date notifications.
(See @ui/dashboard_main.py#1728-1747.)
If the helper throws, it falls back to a direct UPDATE category_commitments SET is_paid = 1, last_paid_date = ? plus notification, then repeats the same refresh calls.
(Fallback path @ui/dashboard_main.py#1751-1775.)
Data layer (commitment_manager)
Method: mark_commitment_paid_manually(user_id, commitment_id) inside core/commitment_manager.py.
It performs the actual database update (sets is_paid = 1, stamps last_paid_date), adds a notification entry, and returns whether the row was updated.
(See @core/commitment_manager.py—exact lines not shown above but this is the helper being invoked.)
Metrics recompute
Because 
refresh_metrics_cards_main()
 leads to 
metrics_carousel.refresh_metrics_cards()
, the dashboard reruns 
load_real_data()
, which re-fetches the Plaid balance and recomputes unpaid commitments and price_after_commitments.
(Refresh logic @ui/dashboard_main.py#2488-2512, balance math earlier in the file.)
So “Mark as Paid” is: UI method → core helper updates category_commitments → dashboard refresh sequence → metrics rerun in 
load_real_data()
 → the cards show the reduced unpaid total.

